                    "text": "I have all the information I need. Let me analyze the root cause.\n\n**The cause of the T-pose flash**\n\nThe playable graph in `AnimaClipsData` is a *weight-blended* graph. `RootMixer` blends three sources, so the total input weight must always sum to 1. The final pose is `movePose * w0 + attackPose * w1 + commonPose * w2`.\n\nThe problem is in `PlayAttackClip` (AnimaClipsData.cs:177-193): it creates the playable, then calls `ResetAttackAnimationTime(attackInputPort)` **before** connecting it to the mixer. At that moment the port is either empty (first attack) or still holds the *old* playable from the previous combo hit — so the reset either does nothing or resets the stale playable. Worse, the whole function is written as \"disconnect then connect\", which leaves the mixer degenerate (all inputs at 0) for the frames in between, and the same pattern is duplicated in `StartAttackEnd`.\n\nBut the actual moment you see the flash is `StartAttackEnd`. Look at the sequence:\n\n```cs\nif (AttackMixer.GetInput(attackEndInputPort).IsValid())\n{\n    AttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackEndInputPort, 0f);\n    graph.Disconnect(AttackMixer, attackEndInputPort);   // mixer now has 2 inputs\n}\n\ngraph.Connect(CachedEndClipPlayable, 0, AttackMixer, attackEndInputPort);\nAttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackEndInputPort, 1f);\n```\n\n`attackEndInputPort` is port 1 of a 2-port mixer. After `Disconnect` the port is *empty* but the mixer still counts 2 inputs, so the \"missing\" port contributes nothing to the normalized blend. Between the disconnect and the connect, plus during the frame where `UpdateBlend` hasn't yet ramped `RootMixer` weight 1 up to 1, the attack branch yields a degenerate/partial pose — and since the graph is likely evaluated before the ramp reaches full weight, the skeleton snaps to the rest pose (T-pose).\n\nThere are two additional contributing bugs:\n\n1. `CachedEndClipPlayable` is created inside `PlayAttackClip`, not `StartAttackEnd`. So the end clip's time already sits at 0 but the playable was created many frames ago and `ResetAttackAnimationTime(attackEndInputPort)` at that time reset nothing (port was empty). The end clip therefore enters at an arbitrary leftover time.\n2. `CachedAttackClipPlayable` is never disconnected after the end clip starts, so it stays connected at weight 0 — harmless, but the disconnect/reconnect churn is what generates the degenerate frame.\n\n**The fix**\n\nStop disconnecting/reconnecting entirely. Keep both ports permanently connected to persistent `AnimationClipPlayable`s, and switch only the clip asset + weights. `AnimationClipPlayable.SetApplyFootIK`/`SetClip` lets you swap the clip without touching graph topology:\n\n```cs\npublic void PlayAttackClip(AnimationClip attackClip, AnimationClip EndClip, PlayableGraph graph, float speed = 1f)\n{\n    if (attackClip == null || EndClip == null) return;\n    if (!AttackMixer.IsValid()) { Debug.LogError(\"AttackMixer未初始化！\"); return; }\n\n    // 端口保持常连，只替换 clip 资产，避免断开连接产生退化帧\n    if (!CachedAttackClipPlayable.IsValid())\n    {\n        CachedAttackClipPlayable = AnimationClipPlayable.Create(graph, attackClip);\n        graph.Connect(CachedAttackClipPlayable, 0, AttackMixer, attackInputPort);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        CachedAttackClipPlayable.SetClip(attackClip);   // 换 clip，不重建拓扑\n    }\n    CachedAttackClipPlayable.SetSpeed(speed);\n    CachedAttackClipPlayable.SetTime(0);\n    CachedAttackClipPlayable.SetDone(false);\n\n    if (!CachedEndClipPlayable.IsValid())\n    {\n        CachedEndClipPlayable = AnimationClipPlayable.Create(graph, EndClip);\n        graph.Connect(CachedEndClipPlayable, 0, AttackMixer, attackEndInputPort);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        CachedEndClipPlayable.SetClip(EndClip);\n    }\n    CachedEndClipPlayable.SetSpeed(speed);\n    CachedEndClipPlayable.SetTime(0);\n    CachedEndClipPlayable.SetDone(false);\n\n    AttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackInputPort, 1f);\n    AttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackEndInputPort, 0f);\n    SetRootTarget(0f, 1f, 0f);\n}\n\npublic void StartAttackEnd(PlayableGraph graph, float speed = 1f)\n{\n    if (!AttackMixer.IsValid()) { Debug.LogError(\"AttackMixer未初始化！\"); return; }\n    if (!CachedEndClipPlayable.IsValid()) return;\n\n    // 同帧内完成权重切换：结束端口先到位，攻击端口再归零，中间没有任何权重和为 0 的帧\n    AttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackEndInputPort, 1f);\n    AttackMixer.SetInputWeight(attackInputPort, 0f);\n\n    SetRootTarget(0f, 1f, 0f);\n}\n```\n\nAnd in `Initialize`, the mixer is created with bare playables absent — add a zero-weight placeholders so the ports are never empty from the start:\n\n```cs\nAttackMixer = AnimationMixerPlayable.Create(RootGraph, 2);\nattackTargets = new float[2];\nattackTargets[0] = 1f;\n```\n\nNow I'll check the callers before editing."
